{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792268666,"nonce":"J6h2I7yGbXbsQGBv25aKkEdqLqY=","title":"Python: isinstance on a value of one of several classes is always False","message":"`isinstance(v, C)` with `v` typed as a class set (`Classes`, an instance of one of several unrelated classes held by address) read the class tag with `zb_any_kind`, as if `v` were a box, so the test never held:\n\n    class Vec(object): pass\n    class Pt(object): pass\n    def pick(i): return Vec() if i % 2 else Pt()\n    a = pick(0)\n    print(isinstance(a, Pt))   # CPython True, zypy False\n\nThe tag must be the instance's own `$class` field. A value typed as one class or a class set may also be None (the null address), and `isinstance(None, C)` is False, so the field is read only when the address is not null. Operators on class sets dispatch with the operand still typed (54b2ab2 follow-up), so `Vec.__sub__`'s `isinstance(other, Pt)` returned the wrong class there.","files":null}]}